, "environment": 8.1, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.9}, "rating_margin": -11.67, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.84, "baseline_margin": -6.05, "analyst_adjustment": -1.95, "key_factors": ["Incumbent Mike Flood holds a large cash advantage ($1.6M on hand vs $290K)", "Raters cluster at Likely/Solid R, though two rate it Lean or softer", "A September Democratic-sponsored poll shows a one-point race, but May polling had Flood up 7-8", "R+6 district lean and a favorable Republican environment offset Democratic energy"], "rationale": "Flood's incumbency, fundraising edge, and a mostly Solid/Likely R rating consensus point to a clear but not insurmountable Republican advantage in an R+6 district. A recent Democratic poll showing a tied race and an active debate calendar justify keeping the margin tighter than the rating consensus implies, but markets still price Flood around 82%.", "watch": "Whether post-debate polling confirms the September tied-race survey or reverts to Flood's earlier 7-8 point lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Mike Flood faces Democrat Chris Backemeyer in a Republican-leaning eastern Nebraska district. Flood has raised nearly $2.4 million and holds a large cash advantage, while most expert raters place the race in the Likely or Solid Republican column. Prediction markets give Flood roughly an 82% chance of winning.\n\nThe race has drawn more attention than expected: Backemeyer has raised about $590,000, and a September poll from a Democratic firm showed the candidates essentially tied. The two met in their only debate in late September, clashing over Iran, tariffs, Medicaid, and immigration. The fundamentals still favor Flood, but the contest appears closer than a typical R+6 incumbent race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margi